# Cloudflare redirect rules, in order; the first match wins.
#
# THE TRAILING SLASH IS NOT CANONICAL. Every page is a real file, so the host
# answers /templates/classic from templates/classic.html and every canonical
# link, sitemap entry and internal href is written without a slash. It also
# answers /templates/classic/ - with its own default, a 307 - and a crawler
# that meets the slashed form keeps it as a second address for the same page:
# 57 URLs sat in the index as "alternative page with proper canonical tag",
# which is crawl budget spent on duplicates. A 301 says the slashed form is
# not coming back, which is the truth: it has never been a page of this site.
/templates/          /templates          301
/examples/           /examples           301
/prompts/            /prompts            301
/templates/:id/      /templates/:id      301
/examples/:slug/     /examples/:slug     301

# The app's own routes are NOT listed here, and the reason is worth keeping.
# They used to be rewritten to the content-free shell (`/resume/*
# /shell.html 200`) so the landing copy inside index.html could not flash
# while the bundle loaded. This host does not implement the 200 rewrite - it
# serves only real redirects - so it turned each rule into a 307 to /shell,
# and every saved résumé's own URL answered with the 404 page. Reloading the
# editor, sharing a link to a résumé and the print route were all broken in
# production by a rule meant to smooth a flash.
#
# Without them the host's single-page-application fallback serves index.html
# for those paths, which is what a router needs, and the boot splash covers
# the pre-rendered landing copy until React replaces it.
